Z||000012||rockcrawl||10-02-2001||04:52 PM||||AOL cds make great discharge terminals for my Tesla Coil! Pumping a few million volts through the disc and having 3 foot sparks coming off the edges makes all the shiny stuff fly off into the air like glitter! It's funny, you can turn the power down and watch the arc take a different path across the disc until it's all burned off. They sure don't last long though, that's why I never complain about getting so many of them.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000013||88CoupeV6||10-02-2001||05:10 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rockcrawl:<BR><B>AOL cds make great discharge terminals for my Tesla Coil! Pumping a few million volts through the disc and having 3 foot sparks coming off the edges makes all the shiny stuff fly off into the air like glitter! It's funny, you can turn the power down and watch the arc take a different path across the disc until it's all burned off. They sure don't last long though, that's why I never complain about getting so many of them.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Cool, I will have to try it with my VDG. If it doesn't have enough power it will give me a good excuse to get a Tesla coil  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>||||reg||7
Z||000014||Ken Wittlief||10-03-2001||09:45 AM||||Ill tell you somthing you can do with an old CD that Ill bet no one has ever seen before (I discovered it my self - patent pending)<P> take a dremil tool, put the drum sander bit on it with a worn sanding drum.<P> Put the CD on the drum, so it just catches the top of the sandpaper and will come off sorta easy (this takes a little trial and error)<P>  hold the cd about 1/2" away from a smooth plastered ceiling, rev the dremil tool up to about 10,000 RMP [warning - dont use a cracked cd, and dont let the edge point at anyone - when they break they explode - but thats not what this is all about]<P>  if the cd is loose enough onthe drum, when it gets up to about 10,000 rpm it will jump up and stick to the ceiling - and keep spinning<P>  then it will scoot all around the ceiling, stay stuck while it slows down gradually.<P> Ive had them stay up there for about 15 seconds. Its the coolest damn thing you ever saw! It creates a vacuum between the CD and the ceiling, but it also maintains a distance of a few thousands of an inch, so it doesnt actually touch the plaster.<P>  Try it! be carefull - if the disk breaks its really violent - but a good disk will usually withstand 10,000 RPMs for a few times.||||reg||10
