Z||000026||8Ball||10-04-2001||07:43 PM||||Ok I am ready for the flames but....<BR>The shop guys and myself were wondering just how much buisness we recieve from AOL problems only. Last year the shop did $184,516.74 in AOL work only. that was 63% of our repair work all year long. We are very anal about records at the shop. We sort our work orders by about 30 differant fields.<BR>Any tech will tell you AOL is the worst pos ever put on a cd for all the nasty things it can do to your system. What online service you use is your choice, but around here aol costs more a month and it costs more in repair bills. $65 an hour at an average of 2 hours per go figure  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Ok flame away but numbers don't lie||||reg||1
Z||000027||Mach10||10-04-2001||09:44 PM||||AOL is garbage. Been there, done that. Useless content, catered to people that don't feel like LEARNING how to use the 'net. C'mon... Internet Explorer is the same thing without the AOL logo, and infinitely more powerful...<P>(Thus ends the bitter words of a tech support agent)<P>Well, at least that's the impression I get from all the AOL users *I* talk to. I'm sure there are some legitimate reasons for using AOL. I've just never found them, either from personal experience, or WOM. <BR>One thing, though. AOL tech is VERY good at deflecting any form of responsibility for their autistic or turrette's Syndrome software (haven't decided of which AoL6 is suffering).||||reg||13
Z||000028||Boomtastic||10-04-2001||10:01 PM||||I cannot see paying for an Internet that's censored, which is what AOL does. You don't get full Internet access (some sites are banned). Plus, as a Systems Admin., I agree that it wreaks havoc with networking connections and DUN settings. And let's not even talk about how insecure their IM software is .... anyone that sends personal information through Instant Messanger does so at their own risk. All information passed on IM goes through *unencrypted* servers! 'Nuff said?<P>But to get back on topic (sorta), I was pleased when they started shipping their CDs in DVD cases. Strip their promotional BS from the cases and you've got a sturdy mailer for shipping your own CDs out.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Boomtastic<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mirror_md_clr.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by Boomtastic (edited 10-04-2001).]||||reg||8
Z||000029||LZeitgeist||10-04-2001||10:35 PM||||AOL has always done everything I've ever needed it to... always has, once they expanded their system about 5 years ago. <P>Never had any problems with my computer specifically related to AOL being on it... <P>More people that I know than I can count have gone through the Mindspring/Earthlink/RoadRunner/ISP-of-the-week problems... I've been with AOL for about 7 years now, and I see no reason to switch. It does what I need it to do. I don't even see the need to upgrade to AOL 6.0 yet. No ISP is perfect - use the one that does what you need it to and stick with it. <P>AOL just seems to be the scapegoat for more problems than it could possibly be related to... and besides, anyone who judges me solely on my ISP doesn't have an opinion worth caring about anyway, so... &lt;shrug&gt;||||reg||1
Z||000030||Mach10||10-04-2001||11:30 PM||||heheheh....<BR>I'm tech support. I get to judge EVERYONE by the sound of their voice, and the nature of the problem  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Seriously though, there's nothing inherently WRONG with AOL, the idea of it, or the implementation of it. It's not the internet, never claimed to be. Its a group of user-restricted web pages, all interconnected... Search engines OPTIONAL  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> If the 'net was as easy to navigate, well, stuff would be easier to find... <BR>I HATE that no-one's figured out how to make an INTELLIGENT search engine viable... "Lessee... &lt;5ex pistols fan site&gt;&lt;enter&gt;.... Found: 1,233,123,543,234 sites found... First link "S3X with PISTOLS; Free streaming Video, bestia1ity FANs, go to this site![popup][popup][popup][popup]"<P>My biggest beef is with the progressively worsening operation of the AOL software. You see, to make things EASY for the customer, AOL changes all sorts of things arbitrarily to make the system compatible with it. It then simplifies the task (ie., doesn't prompt you) of making it the default everything. Then the software corrupts itself, and there's no reliable way of uninstalling it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_watchout.gif"><BR>My other beef is with the email system. They might have changed it, but before, it didn't use POP or IMAP. It used it's own proprietary system. This system had HUGE inherent flaws (like the software) that, while it'd deliver consistant service WITHIN the network, incoming/outgoing email from outside networks would not work. And of course, the tech agents were instructed to treat it like everyone else's problem, until an outage was verified by the NOC. "Hey, it works fine on OUR end, must be the other ISP." At this point, I'd get 2 irate calls. One from the AOL user complaining about not getting email from our network, and one from the other user, OUR user, complaining that they couldn't send to AOL...<P>*changed my examples to some ascii... Don't want them spiders targetting Cliff  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">*||||reg||10
Z||000031||Jaygee79||10-04-2001||11:41 PM||||I've never had a problem with AOL in the past.  It's only been in the past few weeks that I have been getting kicked offline a lot, mostly when I'm downloading something.  That's annoying, but I can live with it as long as it doesn't last forever.  I always get on right away and if I have trouble with something loading (which is almost never) I just pop over to Internet Explorer.  The past few days, though, AOL has been signing itself on.  That drives me nuts.  I sign off and go in the other room. Then I come back in and its online again.  Bugs me out.||||reg||1
Z||000032||Mach10||10-05-2001||12:14 AM||||JayGee: sounds like something's trying to access the net. Suspect a virus/trojan. The other possibility is that AIM is trying to update. I know that AIMSTER does this with gay abandon  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><BR>||||reg||4
Z||000033||LZeitgeist||10-05-2001||06:55 AM||||Not sure why people keep saying that "AOL isn't the Internet". I can type the same addresses anyone else can into the IE browser, or I can just go to <A HREF="http://www.netscape.com" TARGET=_blank>www.netscape.com</A>  to do a search, or yahoo, or anyplace else... not sure what the heck you guys are looking for that "AOL won't allow you to find it"...<P>&lt;shrug&gt; Like I said, use what works for ya... (oh, one other thing I like about AOL is that the mail won't open any attachments unless you specifically tell it to - so Lord knows how many viruses I've avoided thanks to that feature...)<P>Oh well - back to Fieros...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000034||Jaygee79||10-05-2001||12:15 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B>JayGee: sounds like something's trying to access the net. Suspect a virus/trojan. The other possibility is that AIM is trying to update. I know that AIMSTER does this with gay abandon   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I downloaded a program the other day and it's only done it 1 or 2 times I think since then.  I scanned the program with Norton and nothing was found.  The program runs off the internet though so maybe it's trying to sign itself on.  Either that or it's Aimster  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000035||LZeitgeist||10-05-2001||12:32 PM||||I remember that Napster would sometimes try to connect itself unless it was shut down using the 'Exit' command... if you just shut it down by closing the window, then it didn't completely disable the program for some strange reason.||||reg||1
Z||000036||Fiero5||10-05-2001||02:55 PM||||AOL and computer/Network hassle? Let me just say three words: Crash and Burn!<BR>What a pain in the butt AOL is on Windows systems. Especially AIM (Aol Instant Messenger).<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.aol.com/gr/homepage/aolanywhere.gif"> <P>It's everywhere allright! Sheesh!<P>Steve<BR>||||reg||9
Z||000037||LZeitgeist||10-05-2001||03:34 PM||||Works fine for me running Win98... of course, I'm not on a network of any kind, just a stand-alone computer with a dial-up modem, so I can't say how it affects networked systems...||||reg||1
